ER_SCHOOL_NAME) ⋈ SIS_DEPARTMENT (IS_DEGREE_GRANTING, DEPARTMENT_NAME) ⋈ SIS_COURSE_DESCRIPTION (COURSE_LEVEL) for the stats CTE; TIP_SUBJECT_OFFERED ⋈ TIP_DETAIL (ISBN) for the material CTE; join the two CTEs on DEPARTMENT_NAME = OFFER_DEPT_NAME (legitimized by column_concept_department_name: same academic value domain).\n\n**6b.** Two CTEs per the gold decomposition, adding `AVG(tso.NUM_ENROLLED_STUDENTS)` to the second CTE (the >2-enrolled population that \"those subjects\" refers to). Caveats: the SIS_COURSE_DESCRIPTION join fans out per graduate course row (matches dw_1165 precedent, which accepts this); the TIP_DETAIL join fans out per material row, so AVG is material-row-weighted — consistent with the precedent style (COUNT(DISTINCT SUBJECT_ID), MIN(ISBN) over the same join). `VARIANCE()` = population variance in MySQL, as in dw_1165. No rounding applied.\n\n**6c.** Data perspective: all required columns physically exist per retrieved column definitions. Semantic-network perspective: dw_1165's SQL is a verbatim precedent for CTE 1; join keys match documented FK descriptions. Business perspective: the TIP domain is the documented source for ISBN + textbook-program enrollment questions.\n\n**6d.** Alternative: placing AVG in the first CTE (degree-granting/graduate population). Rejected because \"for those subjects\" grammatically attaches to the >2-enrolled course-material clause, and the column mapping lists \"average number of enrolled students\" alongside the second-block mappings.\n\n",
